	(text "Buck converter 12V -> 4.2V\nLT8609 reference implementation\nVOUT == 0.782V x (1 + S6R3/S6R2) == 4.24V\n3-resistor divider added 28/08 to switch off if the supply falls below 3.3V\nuses hysteresis to avoid powering back on until the supply is over 4.6V\nrevised spec of resistors to include anti-sulfur parts"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 179.705 173.355 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "907c40dc-d125-449e-98a0-5cbda786529e")
	)
	(text ""
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 88.9 24.765 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "9a485d4a-1cce-4ddb-9066-8132a5c8c25b")
	)
	(text "3.3"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 268.478 193.294 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(face "KiCad Font")
				(size 1.524 1.524)
				(thickness 0.1905)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "bd537b67-b98f-469a-9fdb-355a655016d7")
	)
	(text "Output voltage divider:\n\n0.782V x (1 + S6R3/S6R2) == 4.24V\n\n\n\n\nEnable divider:\n\nEN threshold: 1.05V rising, 1.00V falling.  VOUT = 4.24V\n\nK = 1 + S6R4/S6R5 + S6R4/S6R6\n\nVIN_on  = K x 1.05                            -> 5.37V\nVIN_off = K x 1.00 - S6R4 x VOUT / S6R6   -> 4.29V\n\n\n\n\nNote: VIN_off may not satisfy ISO 16750-2. Bench tests using a Riden RD6006 power supply,\na v3.1 board and firmware modified to deliberately peak the modem current (CONFIG_APP_LTE_POWER_TEST=y) for 10 seconds\nshowed reliable operation at 3.38v+, consistent failure at 3.36v and non-deterministic failure at 3.37v.\nCurrently unclear if the gap to 4.29V is sufficient to allow the hysteresis to work.\n\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 18.415 116.84 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "c9b4f394-31f7-4628-a607-4dd40e33c7d9")
	)
	(text "BUCK CONVERTER 12V -> 4.2V"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 114.3 19.685 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "e3067861-81c2-44d1-a032-39bd2bb0b1b6")
	)
	(text "ANTI-SULFUR RESISTORS (AEC-Q200 recommended)\n\nS6R2 - FB divider, bottom. If this fails open the output voltage collapses. Safe failure mode but the BOM part is already anti-sulfur so advise keeping it to match the bottom half of the divider.\nS6R3 - FB divider, top. If this fails open the LT8609 sees no feedback and drives output to maximum leading to over-voltage downstream. Mitigated by OVP in sheet 11 but wise to use anti-sulfur here for defence in depth.\nS6R4 - Enable divider, top. If this fails open the board doesn't enable. Safe failure mode but bricks until resolved so fitting anti-sulfur here is sensible.\nS6R5 - Enable divider, bottom. If this fails the device may turn on in brown-out input voltage territory causing unexpected behaviour. Safe failure mode but still wise to use anti-sulfur.\n\nS6R3 is the only critical one for anti-sulfur, the others are recommended but fail safe.\n\nConformal coating is also recommended after build."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 18.415 158.75 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "f74c5785-3594-45c4-a6e1-506309bd81c2")
	)
